{"kind":"artifact","text":"SKILL.md at exact clean pushed head ae847f974ada528b5a4682552e63dce68d948554 on request/skill-notes-and-decisions, answering f49dec8246. This is the implementation report for part one of the notes-and-decisions design, whose note is merged at 155926d3 and whose artifact c2e4a758 is live and unstale.\n\nThe section documents only mechanisms that exist today and adds no code. It states the lifecycle from published revision through adoption to implementation and revision; the rule separating revision from replacement, which is that you amend in place while it is the same decision and write a new file stamping the old one when the decision changes; what belongs in gs merge --text, namely plain English written from the action log for a reader who will never see an event id; and the adoption order, propose and ratify before the review request so the verdict, receipt and successor artifact all reach the adoption through one edge.\n\nThe three facts the six review rounds bought are stated the right way round, and I verified each at source rather than trusting the note or my writer. An artifact cannot be ratified: kinds.go 174 declares KindArtifact with SatisfierNone, so the section says the thing ratified is a proposal and never the artifact. A proposal is adopted by an actor holding ratifier: kinds.go 161 declares KindPropose with satisfier role:ratifier. A verdict is ratified by the review requester alone: kinds.go 170 declares KindReport with SatisfierOriginatingRequester, and the section says the review requester and only them.\n\nThe section makes no claim about staleness at all. That is deliberate. I asserted a false staleness rule in this same file earlier today, it reached normative text, and codex caught it; the safest way not to repeat that was to write nothing near it. A reviewer can confirm by grepping the section for stale and finding nothing.\n\nBoth of the note's open questions stay open. The section does not say whether a note resting on another note learns that from the commit trailer or the front matter, and it does not touch whether watch globs need a second verb. One passage sits close enough to the first that a reviewer should judge it rather than discover it: the replacement paragraph says a new file's front matter names its predecessor by path. That describes replacement rather than resting-on, which is a different relationship, and the same passage says front matter is branch-controlled input that retires nothing by itself, so it grants front matter no authority and settles nothing. If you read it as settling the question, say so and I will narrow it.\n\nAGENTS.md is untouched, as the conditions required, because it governs developing gitseq rather than using a workroom. The diff is SKILL.md alone, forty-seven lines added, nothing restructured, and the Intake passage merged at 7e7dbc88 is neither edited nor duplicated.\n\nOne thing my writer corrected in my own instruction, worth recording because I had it backwards. I told them to place the Rests-On trailer with no blank line before it. Git's trailer parser then read nothing, because the preceding paragraph is prose and a trailer block must be separated from the body by a blank line. The complementary bug bit me earlier today, when a blank line inside the block orphaned one of two trailers. The rule is that the block is contiguous and preceded by one blank line. The trailer on this head parses.\n\nGates at this exact head, all exit zero: git diff --check, go vet, go test ./... -count=1 across thirty packages with no failures, and go test ./internal/docset/ -count=1 run uncached because that gate caches past markdown edits.\n\nSeparately and not part of this head: I filed the adoption proposal for the design itself at cc1f29a4, resting on c2e4a758, because the design prescribes adoption before review and never received one. It awaits hugh, since only a ratifier can adopt, and it blocks nothing here.","body":{"commit":"ae847f974ada528b5a4682552e63dce68d948554","path":"SKILL.md"}}